comments.body:
Thanks for picking up #859, and for the clear write-up. The root-cause notes and the before/after test evidence made this easy to review. I confirmed locally that the three new tests fail on `main` and pass with this change, so the direct `wait` and `callback.get()` cases are fixed.

While trying a few neighbouring patterns, I found some cases where the handler's logs are still dropped after resume. So I don't think this fully closes #859 yet.

**What seems to be going on.** The new transition fires only when the consumed operation was already terminal at the start of the invocation, that is, it completed while the execution was suspended. Some operations are still in flight when the invocation starts and complete *during* it. They don't set `replayCompletedOperation`, so the context stays in replay mode after them, and `DurableLogger` keeps suppressing the new code that follows.

**Cases I reproduced** at `520263cf` with `LocalDurableTestRunner`. Each test records `isReplaying()` right after the operation on the resumed invocation. It gets `true` where `false` is expected:

| Scenario | Result on resume |
| --- | --- |
| `ctx.waitForCallback(...)`. Its WAIT_FOR_CALLBACK child context is `STARTED` at invocation start. | Root still replaying |
| `ctx.runInChildContext(...)` whose body waits | Root still replaying after the child returns |
| `ctx.map(...)` whose branch waits | Root still replaying after `map` returns |
| A step that failed and suspended for its retry delay, then succeeds on resume | Root still replaying after the step |
| Default (virtual) `ctx.withRetry(...)` whose body waits | Root still replaying after it |
| A child context awaiting a parent-created callback (`cb.get()` inside the child) | Child still replaying after `get()`. The parent's flag is changed instead. |

I expect the same mechanism to apply to these, though I haven't run them separately:
- `parallel` branches that wait
- `waitForCondition` completing after a polling delay
- waits, callbacks and invokes that are `STARTED` at invocation start and complete through polling during the invocation

`waitForCallback` is the main callback API, and #859's acceptance criteria call out child contexts and concurrent branches. It would be great to cover these before this lands as "Fixes #859".

**A possible way forward.** The root cause in the description covers one class of operations: terminal at start and listed in `updatedOperationIds`. It doesn't cover the general cause, which is that a context leaves replay mode only when a new operation starts. It may be worth stepping back to the design phase, as CONTRIBUTING rules 8 and 9 suggest. You could restate when a context should leave replay mode, so that one rule:
- covers results that became available while the execution was suspended;
- covers results that become available during this invocation;
- is applied to the context that consumes the result.

Regression tests for the cases above that check what `DurableLogger` actually emits, not only `isReplaying()`, would help lock this in. I've attached the repro below in case it's a useful starting point.

Thanks again for working on this!

<details>
<summary>Repro: <code>sdk-integration-tests/.../InFlightReplayModeTest.java</code> (6 tests, all fail at <code>520263cf</code> with <code>expected: &lt;[false]&gt; but was: &lt;[true]&gt;</code>)</summary>

```java
package software.amazon.lambda.durable;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import software.amazon.lambda.durable.config.StepConfig;
import software.amazon.lambda.durable.config.WithRetryConfig;
import software.amazon.lambda.durable.model.ExecutionStatus;
import software.amazon.lambda.durable.retry.RetryStrategies;
import software.amazon.lambda.durable.testing.LocalDurableTestRunner;

/** Code after an operation that completes during the resumed invocation is new and must not be a replay. */
class InFlightReplayModeTest {
    private final List<Boolean> states = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();

    private void resumeAfterTimer(LocalDurableTestRunner<String, String> runner) {
        assertEquals(ExecutionStatus.PENDING, runner.run("x").getStatus());
        states.clear();
        runner.advanceTime();
        assertEquals(ExecutionStatus.SUCCEEDED, runner.run("x").getStatus());
    }

    @Test
    void waitForCallback() {
        var runner = LocalDurableTestRunner.create(String.class, (input, ctx) -> {
            var v = ctx.waitForCallback("approval", String.class, (id, sc) -> {});
            states.add(ctx.isReplaying());
            return v;
        });
        assertEquals(ExecutionStatus.PENDING, runner.run("x").getStatus());
        states.clear();
        runner.completeCallback(runner.getCallbackId("approval-callback"), "\"ok\"");
        assertEquals(ExecutionStatus.SUCCEEDED, runner.run("x").getStatus());
        assertEquals(List.of(false), states);
    }

    @Test
    void childContextThatWaits() {
        var runner = LocalDurableTestRunner.create(String.class, (input, ctx) -> {
            ctx.runInChildContext("child", String.class, child -> {
                child.wait("pause", Duration.ofSeconds(1));
                return "c";
            });
            states.add(ctx.isReplaying());
            return "done";
        });
        resumeAfterTimer(runner);
        assertEquals(List.of(false), states);
    }

    @Test
    void mapBranchThatWaits() {
        var runner = LocalDurableTestRunner.create(String.class, (input, ctx) -> {
            ctx.map("m", List.of("a"), String.class, (item, index, c) -> {
                c.wait("pause", Duration.ofSeconds(1));
                return item;
            });
            states.add(ctx.isReplaying());
            return "done";
        });
        resumeAfterTimer(runner);
        assertEquals(List.of(false), states);
    }

    @Test
    void stepRetriedAfterSuspension() {
        var attempts = new AtomicInteger();
        var runner = LocalDurableTestRunner.create(String.class, (input, ctx) -> {
            ctx.step(
                    "flaky",
                    String.class,
                    sc -> {
                        if (attempts.incrementAndGet() == 1) throw new RuntimeException("boom");
                        return "ok";
                    },
                    StepConfig.builder()
                            .retryStrategy(RetryStrategies.fixedDelay(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))
                            .build());
            states.add(ctx.isReplaying());
            return "done";
        });
        resumeAfterTimer(runner);
        assertEquals(List.of(false), states);
    }

    @Test
    void defaultWithRetryThatWaits() {
        var runner = LocalDurableTestRunner.create(String.class, (input, ctx) -> {
            ctx.withRetry(
                    "retry",
                    (attempt, c) -> {
                        c.wait("pause", Duration.ofSeconds(1));
                        return "x";
                    },
                    WithRetryConfig.builder()
                            .retryStrategy(RetryStrategies.fixedDelay(2, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))
                            .build());
            states.add(ctx.isReplaying());
            return "done";
        });
        resumeAfterTimer(runner);
        assertEquals(List.of(false), states);
    }

    @Test
    void childAwaitingParentCallback() {
        var runner = LocalDurableTestRunner.create(String.class, (input, ctx) -> {
            var cb = ctx.createCallback("approval", String.class);
            return ctx.runInChildContext("child", String.class, child -> {
                child.step("childStep", String.class, s -> "s");
                var v = cb.get();
                states.add(child.isReplaying());
                return v;
            });
        });
        assertEquals(ExecutionStatus.PENDING, runner.run("x").getStatus());
        states.clear();
        runner.completeCallback(runner.getCallbackId("approval"), "\"ok\"");
        assertEquals(ExecutionStatus.SUCCEEDED, runner.run("x").getStatus());
        assertEquals(List.of(false), states);
    }
}
```

